window.MONSTER_CHUNK_134 = [{"name":"Mummy Golem","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"construct","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":20,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":20,"ac_mods":"+10 natural","hp":64,"hd":"8d10+20","hdvalue":8,"fort":2,"ref":2,"will":2,"dr":"10/magic and slashing or piercing","immunities":["cold","construct traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"2 slams +13 (1d8+5 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d8+5"},{"type":"strangulation"}],"strength":20,"dexterity":10,"wisdom":11,"charisma":1,"baseatk":8,"cmb":13,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":23,"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature appears as a humanoid wrapped in age-worn, filthy bandages. Two glowing red eyes peer from beneath its wrapped form.","group":"Golem","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Strangulation","kind":"Ex","details":"As a standard action a mummy golem can attempt to wrap bandages from its body around a grappled foe's throat. The mummy golem makes a grapple check. If the mummy golem wins the check, it slips several bandages around the foe's throat and deals 1d4 points of strangulation damage in addition to constriction damage. The bandages can come from anywhere on its body, not just its arm or hand. Therefore, a mummy golem can continue to use its slam attacks, constriction, and strangulation attack against other foes. A Medium mummy golem can strangle a maximum of 4 opponents at one time using this ability. A Large mummy golem can strangle a maximum of 6 opponents at one time using this ability. A mummy golem isn't considered grappled while it is strangling an opponent, and can move and attack normally, but it cannot move more than 10 ft. away from an opponent while strangling it."}],"description":"Almost always mistaken for a mummy (and sometimes for an adherer), a mummy golem is comprised of funerary bandages and wrappings sprinkled with mummy dust and held together by magic. The creature has no association with true mummies and isn't undead so it cannot be turned or affected by clerics. Mummy golems are often employed by clerics and necromancers as temple guards or crypt and tomb guards. A mummy golem stands 6 ft. tall and weighs 300 pounds. Its body is composed of tightly knotted and rolled bandages held together by magic (and more bandages). Mummy golems cannot speak or utter any sound. A mummy golem attacks by pounding a foe with its fists. It then grabs an opponent and attempts to wrap its bandages around its throat. If facing more than one foe, a mummy golem attempts to grab and strangle as many of its opponents as possible so it can concentrate on one foe. Mummy golems are often programmed to react in certain ways to particular words or gestures. This programming allows the mummy golem to recoil if a cleric mistakes it for a true mummy and attempts to turn it. While cowering, a mummy golem simply waits for its foe to move within range before lashing out with its slam attacks. Construction A mummy golem's body is crafted from bandages, mummy dust, and exotic oils and spices that cost at least 1,000 gp. MMY GOLEM CL 8th; Price 22,000 gp Requirments Craft Construct, animate dead, geas/quest, limited wish, polymorph any object, creator must be caster level 8th; Skill Craft (weaving) DC 13; Cost 11,500 gp","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Murder-born","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Tiny","type":"undead","subtypes":["incorporeal"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"sense living"}],"auras":["unnatural aura (30 ft.)"],"ac":17,"ac_touch":17,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 deflection, +2 Dex, +2 size, +0 natural","hp":51,"hd":"6d8+24","hdvalue":6,"fort":5,"ref":4,"will":7,"defensive_abilities":["channel resistance +2"],"immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"incorporeal touch +9 (1d6 Cha)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"despondent wail"}],"dexterity":15,"intelligence":14,"wisdom":14,"charisma":16,"baseatk":4,"cmb":4,"cmd":15,"feats":[{"feat":"Blind-Fight","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":9},{"skill":"Fly","value":17},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":12},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":19}],"languages":["Common","any two other languages (cannot speak)"],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","pair"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Appearing from nowhere arises a ghostly image with translucent, yet delicate features. Its unholy eyes rage with absolute and thorough evil that reflects its hatred for the living.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Despondent Wail","kind":"Su","details":"As a standard action, a murder-born can unleash a child-like wail. Any living creature within 60 ft. that hears the wail must make a successful DC 16 Will save or be overcome with supernatural feelings of hopelessness. A powerful curse falls upon the victim and they become so despondent that they need to make a successful DC 16 Concentration check in order to motivate themselves enough to make an attack or cast spell. The curse is permanent, though a remove curse or break enchantment (both requiring a successful caster level check against DC 18) or a limited wish, wish, or miracle can remove the effects of curse. The save DC is Charisma-based. Whether or not the save is successful, an individual cannot be affected by a murderborn's despondent wail for the next 24 hours."},{"type":"Sense Living","kind":"Su","details":"A murder-born can detect living creatures to a range of 100 ft. This functions as a detect evil spell (caster level 8th) except it detects the presence of life rather than a particular alignment. There is no chance the murder-born to be stunned and it isn't blocked by stone, wood, or any other material."},{"type":"Unnatural Aura","kind":"Su","details":"Animals don't willingly approach the creature unless the animal's master succeeds at a DC 25 Handle Animal, Ride, or wild empathy check.."}],"description":"Spawned of hatred when both mother and child are murdered, the rapacious soul of the unborn sometimes rises as a foul and corrupt spirit.\n\nIt is bent on exacting revenge against its killers and against all living creatures it crosses. Driven mad by its unliving existence, the murderborn has blurred its senses into believing that every living creature is responsible for its current state and it never hesitates to attack any that come within range of it.\n\nA murder-born is always encountered within 500 ft. of the place it was killed, and can never move further away than that. It is a solitary creature, though very rarely, when twins are involved, two of these cursed creatures may be encountered together. Neither is aware of the other's presence; they don't communicate, interact, or even see each other.\n\nThey are oblivious to the other's existence. Whether this is because the creatures are so driven with rage it blinds them, or because some magical and mystical force prevents them from interacting is unknown. Murderborns don't speak or communicate but seem to understand a variety of languages.\n\nA murder-born closes quickly when it detects living creatures in its realm. Once it and at least one opponent are within 60 ft. of one another, it unleashes its despondent wail. It then moves to melee range and uses its incorporeal touch to destroy its enemies. Unless driven away or its opponents flee, a murder-born attacks relentlessly until it is destroyed.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Nazalor","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CN","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +5 natural","hp":76,"hd":"8d10+32","hdvalue":8,"fort":10,"ref":9,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +13 (1d8+5), 1 bite +13 (1d8+5)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rage"},{"type":"stun"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":16,"constitution":18,"intelligence":5,"wisdom":12,"charisma":11,"baseatk":8,"cmb":13,"cmd":26,"feats":[{"feat":"Diehard"},{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (survival)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8},{"skill":"Survival","value":8,"special":"+12 scent tracking"}],"languages":["Sylvan (cannot speak)"],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature resembles a bipedal, stocky, bronze furred hyena with slightly oversized claws and fangs.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Rage","kind":"Ex","details":"Three times per day, a nazalor can whip itself into a maddening frenzy. As long as it rages it gains +4 Strength, +4 Constitution, and -2 AC. This rage lasts up to 1 minute, but the nazalor can end it early if desired. It isn't fatigued when its rage ends. While raging, a nazalor has the following changes made to the statistics block above: AC 16 (+3 Dex, +5 natural, -2 rage), touch 11, flat-footed 13; hp 92; Atk 2 claws +15 (1d8+7) and bite +15 (1d8+7); Fort +12; Str 25; Con 22."},{"type":"Stun","kind":"Ex","details":"If a nazalor hits an opponent with both claws and its bite in the same round, that opponent must succeed on a DC 18 (DC 20 when raging) Fortitude save or be stunned for 1d2 rounds. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Nazalors are nocturnal carnivores that derive great pleasure by wading into combat and shredding their foes to pieces with their wicked claws. These vicious bipedal beasts like nothing more than stalking and hunting their prey, toying with it, before finally striking in one swift killing blow. A nazalor is a solitary creature. It is a highly territorial creature and will drive away any creature attempting to lair in what it considers its hunting ground (especially other nazalors). Even during mating season, once the mating ritual is ended, each nazalor goes its own way. Gestation is 7 months and young are born live. They reach maturity within about 15 months. Nazalors feast on anything they can kill, with the exception being one of their own kind; they don't eat other nazalors. These creatures may fight to the death if one moves into another's territory or may fight over a kill, but a nazalor will starve to death before it eats another of its kind. A nazalor stands 7 ft. tall and looks like a stocky, fur covered hyena. Its claws and fangs are large for its build. Its fur is bronze in color with varying shades depending on the creature's age (young nazalors are brighter in color while an aged nazalor's coat is dull). Its eyes are usually green or brown. A nazalor rages into combat swinging its deadly claws and gnashing at its opponents with its sharp teeth. Though the creature can move across the ground on all fours, it always enters and fights in a bipedal stance. A nazalor fights to the death if defending its lair, hunting ground, or if hungry. Otherwise, if combat goes poorly for it, it seeks immediate escape through the quickest route possible (though it may continue to stalk its prey and attack again in the future when it thinks it has the advantage).","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Nithu","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Large","type":"aberration","init":5,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["stench (DC 17, 1d6 rounds)"],"ac":21,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+5 Dex, +7 natural, -1 size","hp":60,"hd":"8d8+24","hdvalue":8,"fort":5,"ref":7,"will":8,"dr":"10/magic","resists":[{"type":"cold","value":10},{"type":"fire","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"4 tentacles +10 (1d8+3 plus grab)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d6+3"},{"type":"disease"}],"strength":16,"dexterity":21,"constitution":16,"intelligence":11,"wisdom":14,"charisma":12,"baseatk":6,"cmb":10,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":25,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":16},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":18},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10},{"skill":"Survival","value":11}],"languages":["Common (cannot speak)"],"environments":["temperate forests","temperate marshes"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"This creature appears to be a semi-translucent and ever-shifting mass of protoplasm floating a few ft. above the ground. Four long and sinewy tentacles writhe and sway from its form. It has no other discernible features.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Disease","kind":"Ex","details":"A creature hit by two or more tentacles in the same round is subjected to mire blindness. Mire Blindness tentacle-injury; save DC 17 Fort; onset 1 day; frequency 1/day; effect blindness and 1d3 Constitution damage; cure 2 consecutive saves. A creature that fails two saving throws is rendered permanently blinded. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Nithus are evil monsters that dwell deep within darkened swamps, bogs, mires, and marshes. These creatures sustain themselves on a diet of swamp creatures: alligators, fish, and adventurers and just about anything else that enters their domain. Slain prey is either devoured immediately or dragged back to the nithu's lair where it is slowly devoured over the course of a few days. A nithu's lair is a well-hidden and dark cave, cavern, or other such place. Nithus occasionally make truces with nearby humanoids (particularly orcs, gnolls, and goblins) in exchange for a steady supply of food (victims).\n\nNithus are solitary creatures and extremely territorial, even fighting their own kind for control of an area. No encounters of multiple nithus have been recorded. Sages are unsure how (and if) nithu reproduce. They appear to be sexless creatures and no young have ever been encountered. A typical nithu has a lifespan of 20 years. As a nithu ages, its color shifts from translucent light gray to a darker, but still semi-translucent gray. Its tentacles, usually the same color as the body change colors as well.\n\nA nithu cares little to nothing for treasure; thus, any treasure found in or around its lair is typically all that remains of those who have fallen prey to the creature's insatiable hunger.\n\nThe nithu is an ambush hunter, preferring to lay in wait for potential prey to pass near. A nithu picks and chooses its targets carefully. The creature's average intelligence is enough to let it to know when it is outmanned and outclassed; thus it prefers to attack the lone straggler or a party member who may have become lost in the monster's swampy lair. Unless extremely hungry, an nithu doesn't attack a large group of creatures unless it itself is threatened and attacked first or unless it is assured of victory.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Nothosaurus","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","init":6,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +6 natural, -1 size","hp":76,"hd":"9d8+36","hdvalue":9,"fort":12,"ref":8,"will":4,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +12 (2d6+9 plus grab)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":22,"dexterity":15,"constitution":18,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":6,"cmb":13,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":25,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Swim-By Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":13},{"skill":"Stealth","value":3,"special":"+11 in water"},{"skill":"Swim","value":18}],"sq":[{"type":"hold breath"},{"type":"speed surge"}],"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","school (3-6)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This grayish-blue reptile has a long sleek body, webbed ft., and long jaws that open to reveal needle-like teeth.","group":"Dinosaur","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Hold Breath","kind":"Ex","details":"A nothosaurus can hold its breath for a number of minutes equal to 6 times its Constitution score before it risks drowning."},{"type":"Speed Surge","kind":"Ex","details":"Three times per day as a swift action, a nothosaurus may take an additional move action in that round."}],"description":"A nothosaurus is about 13 ft. long and grayish-blue in color. When swimming, its uses its webbed ft., fin-like tail, and powerful legs to propel it through the water. It sustains itself on a diet of fish, seals, walruses, and other marine animals. The nothosaurus breathes air and moves slowly on land, rarely venturing far away from the shoreline. When not hunting, a nothosaurus often suns itself on large rocks.\n\nNothosaurus is an ambush hunter, moving quietly through the water as it sneaks up on its prey. As the nothosaurus draws closer, it uses its speed surge to close rapidly and grab the prey with its powerful jaws.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Oliphant","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Huge","type":"animal","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":8,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+8 natural, -2 size","hp":82,"hd":"9d8+45","hdvalue":9,"fort":11,"ref":6,"will":7,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"gore +13 (2d8+13), 2 stamps +13 (2d6+9)","space":"15 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"trample","details":"2d6+13, DC 23"}],"strength":28,"dexterity":11,"constitution":21,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":15,"charisma":11,"baseatk":6,"cmb":17,"cmd":27,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Diehard"},{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Improved Iron Will"},{"feat":"Intimidating Prowess"},{"feat":"Iron Will"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Intimidate","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":9}],"environments":["cold plains"],"organizations":["solitary (male)","family unit (2-8, 25% chance to be accompanied by a calf if 3 or more are encountered)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This massive creature resembles a woolly mastodon with two large downward curving tusks. It appears to be at least 20 ft. long or more.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"The oliphant is a 20-foot long creature resembling a mastodon. It is omnivorous and rarely makes its home near civilized or settled areas. Each of the creature's tusks, if wholly pried from a dead oliphant, brings 400 gp on the market. The oliphant's fur is thick, brown, and aids in protecting it from cooler climates and precipitation. Oliphants are sometimes trained as mounts or beasts of burden by various humanoid races. Oliphants seem to understand Common, but they don't speak it. An oliphant attacks with its downward curving tusks and heavy forelegs; its trunk is too short to be used as an effective weapon. Training an Oliphant An oliphant requires training before it can bear a rider in combat. To be trained, an oliphant must have a friendly attitude toward the trainer (this can be achieved through a successful Diplomacy check). Training a friendly oliphant requires six weeks of work and a DC 25 Handle Animal check. Riding an oliphant doesn't require an exotic saddle. An oliphant can fight while carrying a rider, but the rider cannot also attack unless he or she succeeds on a Ride check. Oliphant young are worth 2,000 gp each. Professional trainers charge 1,000 gp to rear or train an oliphant. Carrying Capacity: A light load for an oliphant is up to 2,400 pounds; a medium load, 2,401-4,800 pounds; and a heavy load, 4,801-7,200 pounds. An oliphant can drag 36,000 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Oliphant originally appeared in the First Edition module EX2 Land Beyond the Magic Mirror (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Queen Thermite","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","subtypes":["extraplanar","fire"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"fire sense"},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":20,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +9 natural, -1 size","hp":67,"hd":"9d8+27","hdvalue":9,"fort":9,"ref":5,"will":3,"immunities":["fire","mind-affecting effects"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to cold"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +9 (2d6+6 plus 2d6 fire plus burn)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"burn","details":"1d6, DC 17"},{"type":"death throes"}],"strength":18,"dexterity":14,"constitution":17,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":6,"cmb":11,"cmd":23,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","environments":["any (Elemental Plane of Fire)"],"organizations":["nest (1 plus 10-40 workers and 5-10 soldiers)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature looks like a giant reddish-hued termite. Immense heat radiates from its body.","group":"Thermite","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Death Throes","kind":"Ex","details":"A queen thermite reduced to 0 or less hit points explodes in a ball of elemental flame dealing 8d8 points of fire damage to all within a 40-ft. radius. A successful DC 17 Reflex save reduces the damage by half. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Heat","kind":"Ex","details":"Touching or being touched by a queen thermite automatically deals 2d6 points of fire damage."},{"type":"Fire Sense","kind":"Ex","details":"A queen thermite notices and locates any fire source equal to a torch or greater within 200 ft. It also senses the intensity of the flame."}],"description":"Thermites are extraplanar vermin that dwell on the Elemental Plane of Fire. They are rarely encountered anywhere else (save the Paraelemental Plane of Heat occasionally). Once in a rare while a gang or band of these creatures stumble through a vortex and sets up their nest on the Material Plane. Such a nest is always located near a massive heat source (such as a volcano).\n\nOn their home plane, thermites build large underground nests. The bulk of the colony is maintained by the workers-whose job it is to construct the nest, forage for food, feed the queen and soldiers, and repair the nest. Soldiers defend the nest against intruders and trespassers. About a week after the nest is finished, the queen takes a king (use the worker thermite's statistics) and they construct a reproduction chamber where she lays 10-20 eggs each day over a period of a week. Young thermites are helpless and depend solely on the queen and workers for food, protection, and so on. Young become independent after several months, and the reproductives (winged thermites) fly off to form their own nests.\n\nThe cycle above then repeats. (Winged thermites have a fly speed of 50 ft. with average maneuverability.) Thermites are red in color and about 5 or 6 ft. long with large biting mandibles and segmented antennae. Soldiers are slightly larger than workers and have slightly larger heads with oversized mandibles. The queen can reach a length of 12 ft. or longer and as she ages, she becomes bloated, resembling a giant reddish colored maggot. Reproductives resemble workers and soldiers but are slightly darker in color and have translucent reddish wings.\n\nThermites are generally passive creatures except when their nest is attacked. In such an event, the soldiers move to defend the young and the queen. Workers join in the melee fighting off any would-be interlopers.\n\nThermites attack by biting with their mandibles. Slain creatures are often dragged into the lair and fed to the queen and the young.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Renzer","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Large","type":"magical beast","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"keen scent"},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +8 natural, -1 size","hp":68,"hd":"8d10+24","hdvalue":8,"fort":8,"ref":8,"will":6,"sr":17,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"bite +9 (1d8+2 plus grab) and two claws +9 (1d6+2)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"command fish"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":8,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":10,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"control water"},{"spell":"water breathing","details":"others only"}]},{"frequency":"2/day","spells":[{"spell":"cone of cold","details":"half; can be used underwater"}]}],"strength":17,"dexterity":15,"constitution":14,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":15,"charisma":14,"baseatk":8,"cmb":11,"cmd":23,"cmd_special":"cannot be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Intimidate","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Swim","value":19}],"languages":["Aquan","Common"],"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A massive, grayish-white shark-like creature swims forth. Its head is horrifically human-like in appearance, but completely hairless, having neither hair, nor eyebrows, nor lashes. Its teeth are long and pointed and its tongue is forked and brown in color. Its shark-like pectoral fins end in three-fingered claws and it has two dorsal fins, side-by-side. Its tail fins curve sharply and are a bit lighter in color than the rest of its body.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Command Fish","kind":"Su","details":"Twice per day, a renzer can command fish and aquatic creatures as the mass charm spell (caster level 8th). The renzer can communicate with any creature it currently commands as if by speak with animals."}],"description":"The deadly renzer roams the seas and oceans preying on fish, aquatic plants, and very often- travelers that enter its domain. Thought to be the result of a wizard's deadly experiment gone awry, the renzer hates most other forms of life and attacks them on sight.\n\nRenzer are a malevolent and vile race and care little for anyone one, including others of their kind. They are solitary, selfish creatures that derive great pleasure from killing other creatures, and so they spend a large amount of time hunting prey; sometimes for food, other times for the thrill of the hunt. Most intelligent races avoid the renzer, but some have actually in recent times opened trade with these creatures. Certain colonies of sahuagin trade goods and services with them, though the deals prove shaky at best. Even the sahuagin don't really like or trust the renzer.\n\nA renzer usually initiates combat with its spell-like abilities, using a cone of cold to blast its foes. In melee combat, a renzer focuses on bite attacks and once it locks its teeth on an opponent, it rarely lets go until either it or its opponent is dead. If fighting a surface-dwelling race or air-breathing creature, a renzer often grabs the opponent and dives straight down, swimming as fast as it can in hopes of drowning the foe. If outnumbered or overwhelmed, the renzer moves a safe distance away and uses its command fish ability.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Rock Troll","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Large","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["earth","giant"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":20,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":19,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +10 natural, -1 size","hp":92,"hd":"8d8+56","hdvalue":8,"hp_mods":["fast healing 5 (while underground)"],"fort":13,"ref":3,"will":1,"weaknesses":["vulnerability to sunlight"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"burrow","speed":20}],"melee":"2 claws +12 (1d6+7) and bite +12 (1d8+7)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rend","details":"2 claws, 2d6+10"}],"strength":25,"dexterity":12,"constitution":25,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":9,"charisma":6,"baseatk":6,"cmb":14,"cmd":25,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Intimidating Prowess"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Intimidate)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Intimidate","value":8},{"skill":"Linguistics","value":-2},{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":3}],"languages":["Common","Giant"],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-4)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This giant creature stands nearly twice as tall as a normal man. Its hide is earth-colored and its hair is dark. Its eyes are deep brown. The creature's arms and legs are long and thin and end in sharpened talons. Its ft. end in three-toed ft.","group":"Troll","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fast Healing","kind":"Ex","details":"A rock troll heals only if it is underground and touching dirt or earth."},{"type":"Vulnerability to Sunlight","kind":"Ex","details":"A rock troll exposed to sunlight (not merely a daylight spell) can only take a single move or attack action and is instantly turned to stone (as if by a flesh to stone spell) in the next round if its fails a DC 17 Fortitude save. This effect is permanent, but can be dispelled if the rock troll is removed from the source of the sunlight and stone to flesh is cast on it. A rock troll must make a new saving throw each round it remains in sunlight."}],"description":"Rock trolls are relatives of the normal troll and make their lairs deep within the subterranean realms of the earth. Most underground creatures avoid rock trolls, as they are completely malign and evil, attacking any living creature, especially when hungry. They are quite fond of human and halfling flesh. Unlike common trolls, rock trolls cannot regenerate lost limbs (though they do possess the ability to quickly heal damage). Rock trolls are 10 ft. tall and weigh about 600 pounds. They resemble their smaller relatives in most respects. The rock troll's hide is stone gray or brown, its hair is black or brown, and its eyes are dull brown. Rock trolls attack any living thing that enters their territory, usually doing so for food. They have no strategy or organization in their attacks; a rock troll flails relentlessly at its foes with its powerful claws until either it or its opponent is dead.","as_character":[{"description":"Rock trolls have racial Hit Dice and can have class levels. Rock troll characters possess the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+14 Strength, +2 Dexterity, +14 Constitution, -6 Intelligence (minimum 3), -2 Wisdom, -4 Charisma. Rock trolls are extremely powerful creatures but are often vulnerable to trickery. Large size. Rock trolls are Large and have the following adjustments -1 penalty to Armor Class, -1 penalty on attack rolls, -4 penalty on Stealth checks, +1 bonus on CMB/CMD, lifting and carrying limits double those of Medium characters."},{"type":"Space/Reach","value":"10 ft./10 ft."},{"type":"Base speed","value":"A rock troll's base land speed is 30 ft. Its burrow speed is 20 ft."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Rock trolls can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Low-light vision","value":"Rock trolls can see twice as far as humans in conditions of dim light."},{"type":"Scent","value":"Rock trolls can detect opponents within 30 ft. by sense of smell."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A rock troll begins with eight levels of humanoid, which provide 8d8 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +6, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +6, Ref +2, and Will +2."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A rock troll's humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 8 x (2 + Int modifier, minimum 1)."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A rock troll's humanoid levels give it four feats."},{"type":"AC","value":"Rock trolls have a +10 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Natural Weapons","value":"Rock troll scan fight with 2 claw (1d6) and bite (1d6)."},{"type":"Special Attacks","value":"rend (2 claws, 2d6+1-1/2 times Str modifier)."},{"type":"Special Qualities","value":"fast healing 5, vulnerability to sunlight."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Rock trolls begin play speaking Giant. Rock trolls with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following bonus languages: Common, Orc."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Sabrewing","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["evil","extraplanar"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"see invisible"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +7 natural","hp":63,"hd":"6d10+30","hdvalue":6,"fort":10,"ref":7,"will":4,"dr":"5/good or silver","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"2 wing slashes +11 (1d6+5/19-20/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"augmented critical","details":"wing slash"},{"type":"lasting wound"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":8,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":13,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"greater magic weapon"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"align weapon"},{"spell":"magic weapon","details":"The sabrewing can use the above effects on its wings to imbue them with the magic qualities bestowed by each of these spell-like abilities"}]}],"strength":21,"dexterity":15,"constitution":20,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":14,"charisma":15,"baseatk":6,"cmb":11,"cmd":23,"feats":[{"feat":"Flyby Attack"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":11},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":11},{"skill":"Knowledge (planes)","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":13},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Survival","value":11}],"languages":["Common","Sabrewing"],"environments":["any (Outer Planes)"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","wing (5-8)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"A 6-foot tall muscular humanoid with black, rubbery flesh stretches two large, leathery wings in place of its arms. Rigid plates of razor-sharp bone jut from the wing's outer edge. It perches on long, muscular legs that end in three-toed clawed ft. Peers menacingly through golden pupil slits, its wide, snarling grimace exposes rows of sharp teeth.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Lasting Wound","kind":"Su","details":"A wound caused by a sabrewing's wing slash doesn't heal naturally and resists healing spells. A creature attempting to cast a cure spell or heal spell on a creature damaged by the sabrewing's wing slash must make a successful caster level check (DC 18), or the spell has no effect on the injured character. The check DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"See Invisible","kind":"Sp","details":"A sabrewing can see invisible creatures as if using the see invisibility spell. This ability is always active. It can be negated, but the sabrewing can restart it as a free action on its next turn."}],"description":"Sabrewings are winged humanoids from an unknown outer plane believed to be formed of rock and lined with razor-sharp blades of all shapes and sizes. These creatures delight in killing those weaker than themselves and often travel the outer planes preying on such creatures when they encounter them. Evil spellcasters and powerful outsiders (demon lords, arch-devils, lesser dukes, and so on) sometimes employ these creatures as assassins because of the sabrewing's skill at killing and the creature's desire to do nothing else. They are not averse to venturing to the Material Plane to answer an evil summons, and often do so, usually with the intent of fulfilling the bargain and then killing the summoner (if possible). If called by a powerful outsider, a sabrewing will not attempt to kill its summoner, as it's smart enough to know when it's outmatched. In such cases, they simply do their job and then return to their native plane.\n\nLittle is known of their native plane, but one adventurer that has supposedly seen this place speaks of huge citadels and fortresses constructed of iron and earth surrounded by \"forests of steel blades.\" This information has never been substantiated and plane jumpers have been unable to locate such a plane. (Perhaps the sabrewings don't actually have their own native plane but implanted such false information to detour those that would seek them out.) Sabrewings prefer to attack from the air, using speed to their advantage while also staying out of the reach of their opponents. Before combat, sabrewings usually cast magic weapon or greater magic weapon on their wings before engaging in combat so they can use their wings to the utmost advantage Multiple sabrewings work in concert with one another to bring down foes; one draws an opponent's attention while another swoops in from behind and slashes with its deadly wings.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Scythe Tree","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Huge","type":"plant","init":-1,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"30 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":7,"ac_flat_footed":19,"ac_mods":"-1 Dex, +12 natural, -2 size","hp":67,"hd":"9d8+27","hdvalue":9,"fort":9,"ref":4,"will":4,"dr":"10/slashing","immunities":["plant traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"3 branches +10 (2d4+6/19-20)","space":"15 ft.","reach":"15 ft.","strength":23,"dexterity":8,"constitution":17,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":13,"charisma":12,"baseatk":6,"cmb":14,"cmd":23,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Improved Critical (branches)","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Lunge"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":16},{"skill":"Stealth","value":3,"special":"+15 in forests"}],"languages":["Sylvan","Treant"],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","grove (4-7)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This many-branched and twisted tree has few leaves on it. In the center of its trunk is a long, deep scar. Its roots are twisted and dark, blackened as if by fire.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Scythe trees are malevolent plant creatures found in heavily forested areas where they naturally blend in with normal trees. Scythe trees are carnivorous by nature and draw very little sustenance from sun, air, or water, preferring a diet of dryad or elf flesh.\n\nA scythe tree, on average, is 20 ft. tall, but may reach or exceed heights of 30 ft. It weighs about 3,500 pounds and has a trunk diameter of 3 ft. Its bark is dark brown, shading darker as it nears the roots. What few leaves a scythe tree has are reddish-brown (and don't change color or fall out as the seasons change).\n\nThe branches of a scythe tree resemble wicked-curving scythes (this is where the tree gets its name). The scar that appears on the trunk is the tree's mouth (it appears as a scar when closed). Scythe trees hate treants and dryads and attack both on sight (though a scythe tree usually only confronts a treant when it outnumbers it).\n\nA scythe tree stands motionless, appearing to be a tree of normal sort, until prey comes within range. It then slashes with its scythe-like limbs attempting to cut its foe to pieces.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Soul Knight","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["fear (30 ft., DC 17)"],"ac":22,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":19,"ac_mods":"+9 armor, +2 Dex, +1 Dodge","hp":68,"hd":"9d8+27","hdvalue":9,"fort":6,"ref":5,"will":6,"dr":"5/bludgeoning","immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"mwk greatsword +12 (2d6+6/19-20)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":18,"dexterity":14,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":10,"charisma":17,"baseatk":6,"cmb":10,"cmd":21,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (greatsword)"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":10},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":15},{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Knowledge (religion)","value":12}],"sq":[{"type":"animated dead"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"(masterwork full plate, masterwork weapon)","description_visual":"A suit of blackened ornamental armor houses two red orbs where eyes should be, and a cold malignance emanates from the creature like an evil cloud.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fear Aura","kind":"Su","details":"The soul knight projects a fear aura that functions like the fear spell within a 30 ft. radius. Affected creatures must make a DC 17 Will save to resist the effect. Use of this ability is a free action. This save is Charisma-based."}],"description":"A soul knight is a suit of armor animated by the lingering soul of an evil knight, cursed to undeath as punishment for having committed betrayal, murder or other crimes. The evil spirit continues to inhabit its old armor, repeating the deeds that brought about the living knight's ruin.\n\nSoul knights seek out those that remind them of their past station (good clerics, paladins, etc.) and attack those foes first in combat.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Original author Mark R. Shipley  Originally appearing in The Black Monastery (© Frog God Games/ Mark R. Shipley, 2011)","gear":"masterwork plate armor, masterwork greatsword"},{"name":"Spawn Of Juiblex","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"ooze","subtypes":["extraplanar"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+6 armor, -1 Dex, +3 natural","hp":80,"hd":"7d8+49","hdvalue":7,"hp_mods":["fast healing 5"],"fort":9,"ref":1,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["amorphous"],"dr":"5/magic","immunities":["acid","ooze traits"],"resists":[{"type":"fire","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20,"special":"30 ft. without armor"}],"melee":"mwk greataxe +10 (1d12+6 plus 1d6 acid) or 2 slams +9 (1d6+4 plus 1d6 acid)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"death throes"}],"strength":18,"dexterity":8,"constitution":24,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":10,"charisma":6,"baseatk":5,"cmb":9,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Blind-Fight"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Iron Will"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":2},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6}],"environments":["any (Abyss)"],"organizations":["solitary","warband (2-7)"],"treasure":"standard (chainmail armor, masterwork greataxe)","description_visual":"This humanoid creature wears chain armor and wields a gleaming battleaxe. Its entire form drips with viscous black slime.","group":"Ooze","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Acid","kind":"Ex","details":"A spawn of Jubilex's acid doesn't harm metal or stone."},{"type":"Death Throes","kind":"Ex","details":"When a spawn of Jubilex is slain, it melts into a pool of caustic acid that quickly covers a 10-foot by 10-foot square area. Creatures within or entering the area must succeed on a DC 20 Fortitude save or take 2d6 points of acid damage each round they remain in the area. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Spawn of Jubilex were once human creatures corrupted by foul and dark rituals at the hands of Jubilex the Faceless Lord. The first known sighting of these creatures was in the Citadel (see G5 Chaos Rising, by Necromancer Games) when Jubilex used his power to corrupt a group of dwarves (known now as The Corrupted). Using his dark magic, Jubilex slayed the dwarves, liquefied their remains, and reanimated them as his spawn.\n\nSpawn are not mindless, but don't retain much of their former lives. Fleeting memories perhaps, but nothing else seems to remain. These creatures are hateful and attack anything they encounter.\n\nSpawn look as they did in their former lives, save they are covered in a thick, black slime that constantly weeps from their bodies.\n\nSpawn attack with weapons, favoring weapons they used in their former life. These creatures rarely retreat from battle, their ever-consuming hatred driving them to their own destruction.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Stirge Swarm","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"magical beast","subtypes":["swarm"],"init":8,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +2 size","hp":78,"hd":"12d10 (+12)","hdvalue":12,"fort":8,"ref":14,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"swarm (3d6 plus blood drain)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain","details":"1d6 Constitution"},{"type":"distraction","details":"DC 16"}],"strength":3,"dexterity":19,"constitution":10,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":12,"feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Improved Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Stealth)"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":17},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":20}],"sq":[{"type":"diseased"}],"environments":["temperate swamps","any underground","warm swamps"],"organizations":["solitary","a pall (2-4 swarms)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Even an animal-level of intelligence is enough for a creature to know there is safety in numbers!","group":"Swarm","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Diseased","kind":"Ex","details":"Stirges are harbingers of disease. Any creature subjected to a stirge's blood drain attack has a 10% chance of being exposed to filth fever, blinding sickness, or a similar disease. Once this check is made, the victim can no longer be infected by this particular stirge, though attacks by different stirges are resolved normally and may result in multiple illnesses."}],"description":"A massive cluster of stirges, the stirge swarm is a band of flying bloodsuckers combining their relatively small personal power to bring down larger foes (and for self-preservation). Stirge swarms are found in the same types of locals as normal stirges; however, they band together when other predators are too powerful or too numerous for the singular stirges to survive.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Originally appearing in Rappan Athuk (© Frog God Games, 2012)"},{"name":"Stone Roper","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"200 ft."}],"ac":22,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":20,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +10 natural","hp":39,"hd":"6d8+12","hdvalue":6,"fort":4,"ref":4,"will":8,"defensive_abilities":["stony hide"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d8+4)","ranged":"6 strands +7 touch (1d4 Strength)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft. (50 ft. with strand)","special_attacks":[{"type":"pull","details":"strand, 5 ft."},{"type":"strands"},{"type":"venom pulse"}],"strength":17,"dexterity":15,"constitution":14,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":16,"charisma":12,"baseatk":4,"cmb":7,"cmd":19,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Point Blank Shot"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (strand)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":12},{"skill":"Knowledge (dungeoneering)","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11,"special":"+19 in stony or icy areas"},{"skill":"Survival","value":12}],"languages":["Terran","Undercommon"],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is a vaguely cone-shaped creature with six tentacles and a large, gaping mouth filled with serrated teeth.","group":"Roper","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Stony Hide","kind":"Ex","details":"A stone roper's tough hide renders it immune to damage from nonmagical ranged attacks, save those of unusually large size (such as boulders hurled by a giant)."},{"type":"Strands","kind":"Ex","details":"Most encounters with a stone roper begin when it fires up to six strong, sticky strands up to 50 ft. away (no range increment). A roper's attacks with its strands are treated as ranged touch attacks. Strands can be severed by any amount of slashing damage (a strand is AC 20). Once a strand has successfully struck a victim, it remains attached until severed or retracted by the stone roper (which it may do as a free action). Attached strands do no further Strength damage, but the struck individual is subject to the pull effect each round, and is subject to the stone roper's venom pulse ability. A creature that begins its turn attached to a strand is numbed and weakened by the strange material, and for each strand attached must make a DC 15 Fortitude save or take 1d4 points of Strength damage. The save DC is Constitution-based, and poison resistances and immunities apply."},{"type":"Venom Pulse","kind":"Ex","details":"Twice per day, a stone roper can exude a pulse of venom through all of its attached strands as a free action. A creature attached to a strand must succeed on a DC 15 Fortitude save or be paralyzed (multiple attached strands to not require multiple saves or increase the save DC). A paralyzed creature appears to have been turned to stone (DC 20 Spot check or close inspection reveals that this is in fact not the case). One round after being paralyzed, the victim recovers and must succeed on a DC 15 Will save or act as if under the effects of a charm monster spell for 2d4 minutes. A charmed creature fights for and defends the stone roper. If the stone roper is killed, a charmed victim acts as if under the effects of a confusion spell for 1d6+2 rounds. The save DCs are Constitution-based, and poison resistances and immunities apply."}],"description":"Stone ropers are distant relatives of the common roper though the two races are don't (as far as sages know) associate with one another. Stone ropers are always found underground and never venture forth to the surface world. They have a dislike for sunlight, though it doesn't harm them in any way.\n\nStone ropers are primarily predators, dining on a diet of fresh meat taken from whatever they can find and kill. When food is scarce, stone ropers become scavengers and dine on carrion.\n\nStone ropers are mottled gray and brown like stone and have a rock-like body. Cutting a stone roper open reveals its treasure (inside its gizzard).\n\nMost encounters with a stone roper begin when it fires its strong, sticky strands. A stone roper attacks anything that comes within 50 ft. by suddenly shooting out its tentacle strands. It prefers to attack two victims at once, each with three strands. The first two victims struck are affected with its venom pulse. If this succeeds, they are immediately released before they can be affected by the stone roper's Strength damage weakening effect. If the venom fails, the stone roper continues to hold the creatures and the weakness takes effect on the creatures' turn.\n\nA typical stone roper stands 5 ft. tall and weighs about 1,000 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Stone Roper (Storoper) originally appeared in the First Edition module A3 Assault on the Aerie of the Slave Lords (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1980) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Stroke Lad","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"fey","init":8,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":22,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +8 natural","hp":57,"hd":"8d6+24","hdvalue":8,"fort":5,"ref":10,"will":7,"dr":"10/cold iron","sr":17,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"slap +8 (1d6+3 plus 1d6 Dexterity drain)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":8,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":11,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"confusion"},{"spell":"dancing lights"},{"spell":"detect evil"},{"spell":"detect good"},{"spell":"detect thoughts"},{"spell":"dispel magic"}]}],"strength":14,"dexterity":18,"constitution":16,"intelligence":15,"wisdom":12,"charisma":15,"baseatk":4,"cmb":6,"cmd":20,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Improved Feint"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":15},{"skill":"Bluff","value":17},{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":13},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":15},{"skill":"Knowledge (local)","value":13},{"skill":"Perception","value":16},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":14},{"skill":"Stealth","value":15}],"languages":["Aklo","Common"],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"A handsome and wicked looking young man of sleight build with sharply pointed ears, small goat like horns jutting from their broad temples. They dress in fine silk robes and ermine lined cloaks and often bear a scepter or some other symbol of nobility.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Dexterity Drain","kind":"Su","details":"Creatures hit by a stroke lad's slap attack must succeed on a DC 17 Fortitude save or take 1d6 points of Dexterity drain. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Stroke lads are a wicked race of fey who make their homes in abandoned manor houses and castles, where they have cursed the masters and chased them from their lands. Stroke lads are vicious in their disdain for mortals showing a special dislike for humans who are often held as serfs to work their newly acquired fiefdom. Stroke lads exact their punishments on servants and enemies who displease them with a touch of their long fingers, withering arms and legs to make one lame, and permanently disfiguring faces of those deemed more handsome than themselves. The slap of a stroke lad is like the kick of a mule. Cowards at heart, they surrender if threatened with death and attempt to negotiate their way out of danger to themselves. Of course they never keep to a bargain and should not be trusted.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Sword Wight","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"LE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":21,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":20,"ac_mods":"+6 armor, +1 Dex, +4 natural","hp":60,"hd":"8d8+16 (+8)","hdvalue":8,"fort":4,"ref":3,"will":7,"defensive_abilities":["channel resistance +4","undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20,"special":"30 ft. without armor"}],"melee":"greatsword +8 (2d6+1 plus energy drain/19-20), or slam +7 (1d4+1 plus energy drain)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":12,"dexterity":12,"intelligence":11,"wisdom":13,"charisma":15,"baseatk":6,"cmb":7,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Blind-Fight"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Toughness"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (greatsword)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":4},{"skill":"Climb","value":7},{"skill":"Perception","value":15},{"skill":"Stealth","value":12}],"sq":[{"type":"sword channel"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","garrison (2-20)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"These wicked and depraved creatures lived and died by the sword, and now, their dark taint passes through their weapons to tear at your soul.","group":"Wight","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Sword Channel","kind":"Ex","details":"A sword wight's energy drain ability functions through melee weapon attacks it makes, in addition to its slam attacks."}],"description":"Much like the standard wight, these undead abominations are warped and twisted caricatures of their former selves. The sword wight bears a massive greatsword, and the cold touch of the grave courses through the creature, through the weapon, into the hapless target.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Originally appearing in Rappan Athuk Reloaded (© Necromancer Games, 2006)","gear":"chainmail, greatsword, gold circlet set with a sapphire (worth 1,000 gp)"},{"name":"Temporal Crawler","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","subtypes":["time"],"init":8,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +1 insight, +2 natural","hp":45,"hd":"6d10+12","hdvalue":6,"fort":7,"ref":9,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["foresight"],"immunities":["temporal magic"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +8 (1d6+3 plus paralysis)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"paralysis","details":"1d4+4 rounds DC 14"},{"type":"slowing webs"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":19,"constitution":15,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":13,"charisma":10,"baseatk":6,"cmb":15,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":24,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Stealth)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":15},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":12}],"environments":["any (Elemental Plane of Time)"],"organizations":["solitary","cluster (2-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A six-foot long hairy, gray spider crawls forth. An hourglass-shaped patch of silver fur dominates its back. Light bounces of its silverymetallic mandibles while its multiple eyes glow bright red.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Slowing Webs","kind":"Ex","details":"The web of a temporal crawler emanates a slow effect (as the slow spell, caster level 6th) that affects all creatures within 10 ft. of the web. Affected creatures can make a successful DC 15 Fortitude save the round they are affected to negate the effects. Creatures are slowed as long as they stay within 10 ft. of a web. The slow effects can be counterspelled with the successful casting of a haste spell if the caster makes a successful caster level check (DC 16) before casting the haste spell. The save DC is Constitution-based. A temporal crawler can fire a web up to four times per day. This is similar to an attack with a net but has a maximum range of 50 ft., with a range increment of 10 ft., and is effective against targets up to one size category larger than the temporal crawler. An entangled creature can attempt a DC 19 Escape Artist check or burst it with a DC 19 Strength check. Both are standard actions. The check DCs are both Constitution-based and the Strength check DC includes a +4 racial bonus. Temporal crawlers often create sticky sheets of webbing up to 20 ft. square. Approaching creatures must make a successful DC 20 Perception check to notice a web; otherwise they stumble into it and become trapped as though by a successful web attack. Attempts to escape or burst the webbing gain a +5 bonus if the trapped creature has something to walk on or grab while pulling free. Each 5-foot section has 10 hit points, and sheet webs have damage reduction 5/ magic."},{"type":"Foresight","kind":"Su","details":"A temporal crawler can see a few seconds into the future. This ability prevents a temporal crawler from being surprised, caught flat-footed, or flanked. It also grants the temporal crawler an insight bonus to AC equal to its Wisdom bonus. This ability can be negated, but the temporal crawler can restart it as a free action on its next turn."},{"type":"Immunity to Temporal Magic","kind":"Ex","details":"Temporal crawlers are immune to all time-related spells and effects (including time stop, temporal stasis, and the like)."}],"description":"Temporal crawlers are man-sized spiders from the Elemental Plane of Time. Upon close inspection, it appears that the hourglass-shaped markings along its back are animated and sand appears to shift from one chamber to the other. If a temporal crawler is slain, the silver hourglass on its back fades to gray and the \"sand\" inside stops flowing.\n\nHow temporal crawlers first arrived on the Material Plane is unknown but sages believe they jumped through a portal to this plane opened by a time elemental or time flayer (see their entry in this book).\n\nThese extraplanar creatures are carnivorous and prey on the flesh of living creatures. They build their lairs near populated areas where the food supply is more than adequate. Temporal crawlers hunt day or night, but prefer to leave their webs at night when they have an easier time corralling their prey.\n\nTemporal crawlers spend most of their time hunting on the Material Plane where prey is easiest to catch. They spin their webs in locations frequented by living creatures and lie in wait. When its prey enters a 10- foot area around its webs and is slowed, the temporal crawler rushes out and attacks. If it can paralyze its prey with its bite, the temporal crawler cocoons it in its web. A creature killed is cocooned and stored as food.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Turtle-shark","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"N","size":"Huge","type":"magical beast","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":21,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +10 natural, -2 size","hp":63,"hd":"6d10+30","hdvalue":6,"fort":10,"ref":8,"will":2,"defensive_abilities":["shell"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +13 (2d6+8 plus grab), slam +12 (1d8+8)","space":"15 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"capsize"},{"type":"swallow whole","details":"1d8+8 plus 1d6 acid damage, AC 15, 6 hp"}],"strength":26,"dexterity":16,"constitution":21,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":10,"charisma":8,"baseatk":6,"cmb":16,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":29,"feats":[{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Swim","value":16}],"sq":[{"type":"amphibious"}],"environments":["any aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","pair"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"From a distance this creature resembles a giant snapping turtle. It has a peaked and craggy shell from which protrude four long fins and a scowling head at the end of a long neck. Rather than a turtle's head, however, this creature's head has irrefutably shark-like features. It has round, black eyes and a gaping maw filled with rows of razor sharp teeth.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Capsize","kind":"Ex","details":"A turtle-shark can attempt to capsize a boat or ship by ramming it as a charge attack and making a CMB check. The DC of this check is 25, or the result of the captain's Profession (sailor) check, whichever is higher. For each size category larger than the turtle-shark's size, it takes a cumulative -10 penalty on this CMB check."},{"type":"Shell","kind":"Ex","details":"If severely threatened, a turtle-shark can completely withdraw into its shell, leaving it almost completely impervious to attack. When withdrawn, a turtle-shark gains a +8 bonus to its Armor Class and a +4 bonus on Reflex saves."}],"description":"A turtle-shark is a giant hybrid animal, which many think is a product of the same twisted magic that created the owlbear. These vicious marine predators have developed a taste for humanoid flesh, and routinely patrol shipping lanes in the hopes of making a tasty meal out of any sailor that has the extreme misfortune of falling overboard.\n\nMuch like a shark's, the teeth of a turtle-shark constantly replace themselves throughout the centuries-long life of the turtle-shark.\n\nIf the belly of a turtle-shark is cut open, it might contain the indigestible belongings of its previous meals.\n\nTurtle-sharks average 20 ft. in length and weigh about 10,000 pounds.\n\nIt shell is dark greenish-brown or dull green mottled with brown splotches.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Undead Mimic","cr":6,"xp":2400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","subtypes":["shapechanger"],"init":5,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +6 natural","hp":59,"hd":"7d8+21 (+7)","hdvalue":7,"fort":5,"ref":5,"will":7,"immunities":["acid","undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10}],"melee":"slam +11 (1d8+7 plus adhesive)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d8+7"},{"type":"soul drain"},{"type":"weeping discharge"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":12,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":14,"charisma":16,"baseatk":5,"cmb":10,"cmd":21,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Toughness","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (slam)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":15},{"skill":"Disguise","value":13,"special":"+33 when mimicking objects"},{"skill":"Knowledge (dungeoneering)","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":15},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11}],"languages":["Common"],"sq":[{"type":"mimic object"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"Two sinewy tentacles sprout from what appears to be an ancient and slime-covered chest.","group":"Mimic","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Adhesive","kind":"Ex","details":"An undead mimic exudes a thick slime that acts as a powerful adhesive, holding fast any creatures or items that touch it. An adhesive-covered undead mimic automatically grapples any creature it hits with its slam attack. Opponents so grappled cannot get free while the undead mimic is alive without removing the adhesive first. A weapon that strikes an adhesive-coated undead mimic is stuck fast unless the wielder succeeds on a DC 18 Reflex save. A successful DC 18 Strength check is needed to pry off a stuck weapon. Strong alcohol or universal solvent dissolves the adhesive, but the undead mimic can still grapple normally. An undead mimic can dissolve its adhesive at will, and the substance breaks down 5 rounds after the creature is destroyed. The save DC is Strength-based."},{"type":"Mimic Object","kind":"Ex","details":"An undead mimic can assume the general shape of any Medium object, such as a massive chest, a stout bed, or a door. The creature cannot substantially alter its size, though. An undead mimic's body is hard and has a rough texture, no matter what appearance it might present. An undead mimic gains a +20 racial bonus on Disguise checks when imitating an object in this manner. Disguise is always a class skill for an undead mimic."},{"type":"Soul Drain","kind":"Su","details":"An undead mimic can take a move action to devour the soul of an adjacent creature it has recently (within the last hour) killed. A creature whose soul is drained cannot be restored to life until the undead mimic containing its soul is destroyed. Each time an undead mimic drains a soul it gains 1d8 temporary hit points and gains a +2 bonus to Strength for 1 hour. These bonuses stack with themselves. After 24 hours, the soul is completely devoured, and the deceased creature cannot be brought back to life via raise dead (resurrection and more powerful effects work normally though the undead mimic must still be destroyed before this magic works)."},{"type":"Weeping Discharge","kind":"Ex","details":"An undead mimic constantly leaks and oozes its foul adhesive in a 20-foot-radius area around its body. The area quickly becomes coated in the fluids and is considered difficult terrain. Undead mimics are not hampered by this secretion (their own or that of another undead mimic). The slimy coating lasts for 1 hour before becoming inert."}],"description":"Undead mimics are believed to be the result of experimentation on mimics by insane necromancers. What possessed them to create an undead version of a truly horrid creature is beyond most scholars' comprehension.\n\nUndead mimics, unlike standard mimics, are evil, poisoned by the necromantic magic that created them. They desire flesh and blood and dine on the souls of those they slay.\n\nUndead mimics don't associate with standard mimics (or most other creatures for that matter). Often times, an undead mimic is found in the employ of an evil necromancer, possibly the one that created it.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""}];
